# Line ranges

A loom arrives as plain text, and Loom reads it into the tree Part II describes. Four passes do the reading, a chapter each: line ranges split the text into lines, the classifier names each line's kind, the tokeniser reads the marks inside each line, and the document builder assembles the document. The fifth chapter builds one module from a loom, its document beside the product composed from it, and the last gathers how every pass reads fenced code.

`LineRanges` is the parser's first pass. Its service, `LoomSourceRanges`, cuts a file's text into ranges, one for each line. A range is a half-open pair of offsets, `[start, end)`. Every later pass slices the text by these offsets, and Volar maps from them. The service detects once how the file ends its lines, and it refuses a file that ends them in more than one way. That is the one failure the parse chain cannot pin to a node.

## Terminator conventions

A file ends its lines one of three ways, named by `EolKind`: a line feed (`lf`), a carriage-return and line-feed pair (`crlf`), or a bare carriage return (`cr`). An `Eol` bundles a detected kind with two regexes — `pattern` to find its own terminator, `stray` to spot any other. `MixedEOL` is the error raised when a file uses more than one; it carries human-friendly line numbers, since mixed terminators are invisible on screen.

=>

export type EolKind = 'lf' | 'crlf' | 'cr'

type Eol = {
  readonly kind: EolKind
  readonly pattern: RegExp
  readonly stray: RegExp
}

export class MixedEOL extends Data.TaggedError('MixedEOL')<{
  readonly primary: EolKind
  readonly found: EolKind
  readonly offset: number
  readonly primaryLine: number
  readonly foundLine: number
}> {}

## Detecting the convention

`detectEol` scans for the first terminator and adopts it as the file's convention, returning nothing for a single line that has none. `eolOf` builds the `Eol` for a kind — the `pattern` that walks its lines and the `stray` that catches a foreign terminator.

=>

export const detectEol = (s: string): Option.Option<Eol> => {
  const lf = s.indexOf('\n')
  const cr = s.indexOf('\r')
  if (lf < 0 && cr < 0) return Option.none()
  if (cr < 0) return Option.some(eolOf('lf'))
  if (lf < 0) return Option.some(eolOf('cr'))
  return cr < lf
    ? cr + 1 === lf
      ? Option.some(eolOf('crlf'))
      : Option.some(eolOf('cr'))
    : Option.some(eolOf('lf'))
}

const eolOf = (kind: EolKind): Eol => {
  switch (kind) {
    case 'lf':
      return { kind, pattern: /\n/g, stray: /\r/g }
    case 'crlf':
      return { kind, pattern: /\r\n/g, stray: /(?<!\r)\n|\r(?!\n)/g }
    case 'cr':
      return { kind, pattern: /\r/g, stray: /\n/g }
  }
}

## Checking for a mix

`checkMixed` confirms a file holds to its convention throughout: it succeeds with the `Eol` when no stray terminator appears, and fails with `MixedEOL` otherwise. `lineOfOffset` counts visual lines up to an offset, treating every terminator kind as one break, so it reports the right line even when terminators are mixed. `strayKind` names the offending terminator from its matched text.

=>

const checkMixed = (text: string, eol: Eol): Effect.Effect<Eol, MixedEOL> => {
  const stray = eol.stray.exec(text)
  if (!stray) return Effect.succeed(eol)
  eol.pattern.lastIndex = 0
  const primary = eol.pattern.exec(text)
  const primaryOffset = primary ? primary.index : 0
  return Effect.fail(
    new MixedEOL({
      primary: eol.kind,
      found: strayKind(eol.kind, stray[0]),
      offset: stray.index,
      primaryLine: lineOfOffset(text, primaryOffset),
      foundLine: lineOfOffset(text, stray.index),
    }),
  )
}

const lineOfOffset = (text: string, offset: number): number => {
  let line = 1
  for (let i = 0; i < offset; i++) {
    const c = text.charCodeAt(i)
    if (c === 10) line++
    else if (c === 13) {
      line++
      if (i + 1 < text.length && text.charCodeAt(i + 1) === 10) i++
    }
  }
  return line
}

const strayKind = (primary: EolKind, match: string): EolKind => {
  if (match === '\r\n') return 'crlf'
  if (match === '\n') return 'lf'
  if (match === '\r') return 'cr'
  return primary
}

## The range of every line

A `LineRange` is a half-open pair of offsets, `[start, end)`, and a line's terminator falls inside the line's range. `LineRangeSchema` mirrors the pair for schema fields, and holds both offsets to non-negative integers.

`lineRanges` gives the range of every line of a text. The convention's `pattern` finds every terminator, and a line ends where its terminator ends. The first line starts at offset zero, and every other line starts where the line before it ended. The last line has no terminator, so it runs to the end of the text. It is empty when the text ends with a terminator.

The text itself is never split or copied. A later pass slices it by a range when it needs the line.

=>

export type LineRange = readonly [start: number, end: number]

const NonNegativeInt = Schema.Number.check(
  Schema.makeFilter<number>((n) =>
    Number.isInteger(n) && n >= 0 ? undefined : 'must be a non-negative integer',
  ),
)

export const LineRangeSchema = Schema.Tuple([NonNegativeInt, NonNegativeInt])

const lineRanges = (text: string, eol: Eol): ReadonlyArray<LineRange> => {
  const ends = [...text.matchAll(eol.pattern)].map(
    (terminator) => terminator.index + terminator[0].length,
  )
  return Array.zip([0, ...ends], [...ends, text.length])
}

## The service

`LoomSourceRanges` is the pass as an Effect service. Its `rangesOf` method detects the convention, checks the text for a mix, and gives the range of every line. It fails with `MixedEOL` when the text mixes terminators. A text with no terminator is a single line, and `rangesOf` gives the one range that covers it.

=>

export class LoomSourceRanges extends Context.Service<LoomSourceRanges>()('LoomSourceRanges', {
  make: Effect.succeed({
    rangesOf: (text: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<LineRange>, MixedEOL> =>
      Option.match(detectEol(text), {
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<LineRange>>([[0, text.length]]),
        onSome: (eol) =>
          checkMixed(text, eol).pipe(Effect.map((validEol) => lineRanges(text, validEol))),
      }),
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## What this module draws on

Effect supplies `Array.zip` to pair each line's start with its end, `Data` for the tagged `MixedEOL` error, `Schema` for `LineRangeSchema`, and `Option` and `Effect` for the rest.

=>

import { Array, Context, Effect, Layer, Option, Data, Schema } from 'effect'

